  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tThe letters A, E, I, O, and U are vowel letters. Put them in the pink vowel box.": "tts_5f7a7e03d5418c.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tThe other letters are consonant letters. In this lesson, Y is grouped with the consonants. Put the rest in the purple consonant box.": "tts_73a045e0c352e8.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tWhat sound does each vowel letter make in a CVC word?": "tts_2ff7c488b8ff04.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tCan you think of a CVC word for each vowel letter A, E, I, O, or U?": "tts_1f8dcaea337fe7.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tIn a closed syllable, the vowel is closed in and usually says its short sound.": "tts_00543e9876fef9.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tWhen two consonants stand between two vowels, we often split between them. There are exceptions.": "tts_727ec4a17a6aa5.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tSyllables are the beats we hear in a word. Great job today!": "tts_8c6f696da8c7af.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tPlease put the vowel letters in the pink box. You will put the rest in the purple consonant box in a moment.": "tts_768ef9b464f6ad.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tThe letters of the English alphabet are divided into two types. What are the two types?": "tts_80deab711c37e0.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tThe two types are vowels and consonants.": "tts_47cc8aa7ef3fdb.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tExcellent! That sorting is correct.": "tts_096c9912ba0a9e.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tNot quite. The pink box should have A, E, I, O, and U. Y belongs with the consonants in this lesson.": "tts_e1d8c21c80c495.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tFeel the beats of the music! Clap your hands with the steady pulse.": "tts_293e9dd19b1c28.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tWhen you clapped, what part of the music were you following? You were clapping with the beat!": "tts_c96eaf1f02ccb1.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tBeat! The steady pulse you feel in music is called the beat.": "tts_fa9c564430269c.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tIn English, we talk about beat in words too — like the steady parts we feel when we say a word.": "tts_3046923acfc40a.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tListen carefully. Clap along with the word watermelon and feel the rhythm inside the word.": "tts_2d029e56ef8042.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tHow many times can you clap for this word? Watermelon has four syllables, so you might clap four times: wa, ter, me, lon.": "tts_acbccc65ed90de.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tThe louder or stronger parts in a word often come from vowel sounds. Each clap can stand for one syllable — one beat in the word.": "tts_f4a09757487274.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tSyllables are the beats of a word. Learning syllables helps us read and spell better.": "tts_775c1dfdb18a1d.mp3",
  "en-GB-RyanNeural\tIn a C V C word, the vowel is closed in by consonants. A usually has a short A sound as in cat. E usually has a short E sound as in pen. I usually has a short I sound as in big. O usually has a short O sound as in hot. U usually has a short U sound as in cup. We call these short vowel sounds.": "tts_7272e6705a8dcd.mp3",
  "zh-CN-XiaoxiaoNeural\t元音字母是 A、E、I、O、U。发元音时，气流从喉咙出来比较畅通，口腔里的阻碍很小。辅音字母是其余的二十一个字母。发辅音时，气流在口腔里会受到阻碍、摩擦或完全阻塞，所以听起来像被「挡住」了一下。": "tts_2fb2b883fa4396.mp3",
  "zh-CN-XiaoxiaoNeural\t辅音就像路上的停牌，气流要停一下，有阻碍。元音像没有停牌的大路，气流更畅通，可以一直往前走。": "tts_c785e05b865048.mp3"
}